 "where": "Proposition 4.25",
 "paper text": [
  {
   "where": "Proposition 4.25 (proof)",
   "text": "Suppose neither $h_b$ nor $bc$ occurs. Contract $cf$ and take a torus embedding of this proper minor. Identify the two boundary copies of $P_1$ in its incident facial disks, keeping all other boundary appearances distinct. The path $P_1$ then lies inside the resulting region apart from its ends. By the rotation table~\\supp{R3}, this region is either the annulus $ac\\mid bd$ or a disk with attachment sequence $a,c,b,d,c$ or $a,c,b,c,d,c$. Every $\\Psi_1$-bridge inside $B$ meets the interior of $P_1$, so lies in this region. No other face contains both $a,d$, so any present $h_a$ or $ad$ lies there too. The annulus would make $H_1$ planar by Lemma~\\ref{lem:annulus-embedding}. The first disk would make $H_1+bc+cd$ planar by Lemma~\\ref{lem:embedding-extension}, so $H_1$ would again be planar. Both contradict its nonplanarity. Thus only the last disk occurs. Joining its consecutive attachments gives the edges $ac,bc,cd$, so $(B\\cup H_{\\mathrm{ext}})+ac+bc+cd$ is planar. Its subgraph $H_1-bd$ is therefore planar, which means every $K_{3,3}$ subdivision of $H_1$, including $H_2'$, uses $bd$. If neither $h_a$ nor $ad$ occurs, contracting $ed$ instead gives $H_1-ac$ planar, so every such subdivision uses $ac$. Construction~\\ref{common:marked-construction} therefore lists every possible $B$ using the six choices of $H_{\\mathrm{ext}}$, the added edges $ac,bd$, and the subdivision edges and planar completions required above. Adjoining $J_1\\cup J_2$, the edges $cf,ed$ and the chosen $H_{\\mathrm{ext}}$ to each candidate gives the graphs to be checked. The checks in~\\supp{E6} leave only $\\mathfrak T_{21}$ and $\\mathfrak T_{63}$."
  }
